@page "/learn/virtualize-csp"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Virtualize under a strict CSP · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Virtualize under a strict CSP</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your Content-Security-Policy bans inline styles. Why do your virtualized lists stop working?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a fix, not a new API. <code>Virtualize</code> no longer writes inline styles,
        so it works when your policy bans them. Your code doesn't change. In .NET 10 the same list broke, with no
        error on screen.
    </p>

    <p>
        A Content-Security-Policy (CSP) tells the browser what a page is allowed to use. A strict one says
        <code>style-src 'self'</code>: styles only come from your stylesheets. Any <code>style="…"</code> attribute
        in the markup is ignored.
    </p>
    <p>
        We built a small .NET 10 app with a <code>Virtualize</code> list of 1,000 test orders, each 40px tall, under
        that policy. Here's the list it rendered:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What .NET 10 rendered, and what the browser did with it</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;div <span class="err">style="height: 0px; flex-shrink: 0;"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;       <span class="err">refused</span>
&lt;div class="row"&gt;Order #1&lt;/div&gt;
<span class="dim">... Order #2 to #18 ...</span>
&lt;div class="row"&gt;Order #19&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div <span class="err">style="height: 39240px; flex-shrink: 0;"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;   <span class="err">refused: 0px</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        This site runs .NET 11, where the bug is fixed. So Fig. 1 serves that recorded markup, with the same policy.
        The markup is recorded, but the blocking is live: your browser refuses the spacers right now.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The order list, replayed from .NET 10" Url="demo/virtualize-csp/orders/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Scroll the list. It says 1,000 orders, but it stops at <b>Order #19</b>. No error on screen.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The spacers use inline styles</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Virtualize</code> only renders the rows you can see. Above and below them it puts an empty
        <code>div</code>, called a spacer, as tall as the rows it stands in for. That's what lets you scroll. In
        .NET 10, each spacer's height was an inline style, as in the markup above.
    </p>
    <p>
        The policy blocks both styles, so the spacers have no height. The list can scroll 760px when it needs
        40,000px. You can never reach Order #20. Nothing throws, and the page doesn't tell you why.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Upgrade. That's it</h2>
    <p>There's no code to change. .NET 11's <code>Virtualize</code> writes its spacers differently:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The same spacer in .NET 11</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">&lt;!-- .NET 10 --&gt;</span>
&lt;div <span class="err">style="height: 39240px; flex-shrink: 0;"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

<span class="dim">&lt;!-- .NET 11 --&gt;</span>
&lt;div <span class="hl">data-blazor-virtualize-reserved-height="39240"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The height is now plain data. Blazor's script reads it and sets <code>element.style.height</code>. A CSP
        only blocks styles written in the markup, not ones set by script, so the height works. Here's a list on
        .NET 11, under the same policy. It isn't the 1,000 test orders: it's this app's own orders, so the count
        differs.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="An order list on .NET 11, same policy" Url="demo/virtualize-csp/orders"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            Scroll the list. It goes all the way to the oldest order. Orders are sorted by date, so their numbers
            aren't always in order. The policy is on here too: the line above
            the list asks to be red, and it stays grey because the policy blocks it.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the code behind both figures. None of it is a change you need to make: the fix is the upgrade.</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/VirtualizeCsp/OrdersNet10.razor" Feature="virtualize-csp">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1: the markup the .NET 10 app produced, recorded, and served live with the same policy as Fig. 2. Highlighted: the two
            spacers your browser blocks.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/VirtualizeCsp/Orders.razor" Feature="virtualize-csp">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page. Highlighted: the policy, <code>style-src 'self'</code> with no <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>.
            This sets up the test. It isn't the fix.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/VirtualizeCsp/OrderList.razor" Feature="virtualize-csp">
        <Caption>
            The list in Fig. 2. Highlighted: an ordinary <code>&lt;Virtualize&gt;</code>, the same as in .NET 10.
            The comment above it shows how its spacers render in each version.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Loosen the policy, or drop Virtualize</h2>
    <p>
        CSP's usual exceptions can't allow these styles. A nonce (a one-time token on a tag) doesn't work on style
        attributes. A hash (a fingerprint of one exact style) needs <code>'unsafe-hashes'</code>, and you'd need one
        for every possible spacer height. That left two choices:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways round it in .NET 10, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">style-src 'self' <span class="err">'unsafe-inline'</span></pre>
                <span class="why">allow inline styles after all; the .NET 10 app then scrolled to Order #1000</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ HTML an attacker slips in can restyle the page, say to hide a warning. Security reviews also flag it</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@foreach (var order in orders)
{
    &lt;OrderRow Order="order" /&gt;
}</pre>
                <span class="why">don't virtualize</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every row is on the page, so a long list is slow to load and to scroll</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Strict policy, virtualized list</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the same app, with the same policy and code, scrolls the whole list with no CSP errors. This works
        whether the list runs on the server or in WebAssembly, and for QuickGrid's virtualized rows too.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>DevTools still shows <code>style="height: …"</code> on .NET 11 spacers.</b> That's the height the script set, not markup. The policy doesn't check it.</li>
        <li><b>Your own inline styles are blocked too,</b> like the dashed line in Fig. 2. Move them into stylesheets.</li>
        <li><b>The policy comes with the first page you load.</b> After that, Blazor swaps each new page into the current one instead of loading it fresh (enhanced navigation), so the browser never reads the new page's policy header. That's why this app's Support screen (<code>/support</code>) also puts its policy in a <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code> tag: Blazor adds it to the page, and the browser applies it from then on.</li>
        <li><b>Lock down scripts too.</b> This demo only restricts styles. A full policy also bans inline scripts. Blazor writes its import map (the list that tells the browser where its JavaScript files are) as an inline script. Allow that one script in the policy by its hash, a fingerprint of its exact text. The Support screen does this.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
